---
// /financing/ — built because the footer links to it, noindex because the terms are not sourced.
//
// The prototype advertised "financing as low as $99/mo", "$0 down" and "low- and 0%-interest
// options" across roughly twenty pages. All of it came from the design comp, none of it from a
// lender. In the US, advertising a specific monthly payment is a "triggering term" under
// Regulation Z: it obliges you to disclose the actual terms alongside it. Publishing a payment
// figure nobody can trace to a credit agreement is the kind of mistake that costs more than the
// leads it wins, so every one of those figures is gated in data/claims.js.
//
// This page therefore says the true, useful thing — financing exists, ask about it at the
// estimate — and says nothing about rates until the lender's own terms are on file.
import PageShell from "../layouts/PageShell.astro";
import { CLAIMS } from "../data/claims.js";
const f = CLAIMS.financing;
---
<PageShell
  reviews={true}
  offer={true}
  title="Financing Your Roof or Exterior Project | Coldstream Exteriors"
  description="Financing options for roofing, siding, window and gutter projects with Coldstream Exteriors. Ask at your free estimate and we'll walk you through what's available."
  canonical="/financing/"
  noindex={!f}
  eyebrow="Financing"
  heading="Paying for the work over time"
  intro="A roof is rarely a planned purchase. Financing is available, and the person doing your estimate can walk you through it."
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The honest position, first</h2>
      <p>
        We are not going to print a monthly payment on this page until we can stand behind it.
        Advertising a specific monthly figure is regulated in the United States — quote
        a specific payment and you have to disclose the lender, the rate, the term and who actually
        qualifies. A number without those attached is not an offer, it is a hook.
      </p>
      <p>
        So this page tells you what is true today: what a job costs depends on the job, the
        inspection and the written quote are free, and there are three sensible ways people pay for
        this kind of work.
      </p>

      <h2>Three ways this usually gets paid for</h2>
      <h3>1. Insurance, when it is storm damage</h3>
      <p>
        This is the one most people underuse. Sudden damage from hail or wind is generally covered;
        age, wear and deferred maintenance generally are not. The distinction matters more than
        most homeowners expect, and it is not always visible from the ground — hail takes the
        granules off a shingle without breaking it, so a roof can look fine from the driveway with
        years taken off its life.
      </p>
      <p>
        We inspect and document damage the way an adjuster needs it documented, meet them on site
        where we can, and deal with them directly afterwards. We will also tell you when we think a
        claim will not go anywhere, because filing one that fails still puts it on your record.
        <a href="/cincinnati/roofing/insurance-storm-damage/">How a storm claim works →</a>
      </p>

      <h3>2. Doing the urgent part now and the rest later</h3>
      <p>
        Not every job has to happen at once. A failing north slope can be replaced this season and
        the rest of the roof next; siding can be done one elevation at a time. It is not always the
        least expensive route in total, but it is often the right one, and we would rather scope it that
        way than watch someone defer the whole thing and pay for decking later.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Financing</h3>
      <p>
        Arrangements are being finalised. When they are, the lender, the terms, the rate and the
        qualifying criteria will be published here in full — not a headline figure with the details
        somewhere else.
      </p>

      <h2>Why waiting usually costs more</h2>
      <p>
        This is the part worth understanding whichever route you take. Water that gets past a roof
        does not stay in one place: it runs along decking and rafters, soaks insulation, and shows
        up on a ceiling some distance from where it got in. A repair that would have been a
        flashing detail becomes decking, insulation and drywall after a winter.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is an argument for looking at it now, not for signing something today. If the honest
        answer is that it can safely wait a season, we will say so.
      </p>

      <h2>What the inspection costs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Nothing to book.</strong> No deposit and no call-out charge.</li>
        <li><strong>Nothing for the quote.</strong> It is written, itemised, and yours to keep — including if you take it to somebody else.</li>
        <li><strong>Nothing to say no.</strong> We will follow up once. If the answer is no, that is the end of it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions worth asking any contractor</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Is the payment schedule in the written quote, or agreed later?</li>
        <li>Who is the lender, and is the rate fixed?</li>
        <li>What happens to the financing if the scope changes mid-job?</li>
        <li>Is the workmanship warranty from the contractor or the manufacturer? They are different promises.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Ask us the same four. If any answer is vague, that is useful information about whoever is
        giving it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageShell>
